\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Discrete induction and its open interfaces}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{How can algebra and geometry organize induction and background
variation? [Gaussian elimination](fdip-000F) gives a finite example;
the [synthesis conjecture](fdip-003X) and
[finite interfaces](fdip-003Y) distinguish this result from the
further maps required.}

\p{The route through these explorations is the diagram opening the
[compatibility test](fdip-009Y). That test fixes one finite spinor
datum. The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain open tests, as does
[what the structure fixes and leaves free](fdip-009G).
The conjecture and finite interfaces below remain distinct from
those open questions.}

\p{The constructions now on this route include finite-group symmetry,
selected backgrounds and connections, finite and compact gauge
fields, reflection positivity, explicit transfer operators, Gauss's
law, Grassmann integration, fermionic elimination, the covariant
Dirac operator and the free Wilson remedy. The arrow from gauge
fields to Euclidean time, drawn in the [reader guide](fdip-004E),
is a reading prerequisite for the [gauge transfer](fdip-007Y) and
for [Gauss's law](fdip-0088). Reflection positivity alone does not
supply that time shift. The arrow from gauge fields to lattice
fermions is a reading prerequisite for the covariant Dirac
operator and for the free Wilson remedy that follows it, not for
Grassmann integration or fermionic elimination, and not an
identification of those operators with a physical electron.}

\p{The comparisons that remain open are these. The
[physical environment map](fdip-003X) #{E} is not constructed.
The [supplied-circle limit](fdip-004J) controls one fixed Fourier
mode; it is not a continuum limit of the full induced law, and no
moduli map controls that limit. The
[enriched background groupoid](fdip-000X) and the
[geometric realization](fdip-004L) do not supply a site, a stack,
or a descent that organizes those families.
The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain open tests.
[What the structure fixes and what it leaves free](fdip-009G)
is still a planned section, and the [Structure](fdip-008R) host
remains planned. None of these absences is a no-go theorem or a
physical identification.}

\p{\strong{Accounting.} Each background unit below is recorded
by the trees it delivered and by the statement that was proved,
at the scope of that statement. The reader-guide units are prose.
They are not rows of this table.}

\table{
  \thead{\tr{\th{Unit}\th{Delivered trees}\th{What is proved, and at what scope}}}
  \tbody{
    \tr{\td{S1}\td{[004R](fdip-004R) to [004X](fdip-004X)}\td{Complete real isotypic projectors for a finite group in an orthogonal representation. The hypotheses are finiteness, characteristic zero and that orthogonal action.}}
    \tr{\td{S2}\td{[004Y](fdip-004Y) to [0053](fdip-0053)}\td{The commutant and the bridge among real, complex and quaternionic types. The prism determinant and the binary-tetrahedral Cayley spectrum are examples. #{L_\Xi} is kept apart from #{Q_\Xi=L_\Xi+m^2I}.}}
    \tr{\td{S3}\td{[0054](fdip-0054) to [0058](fdip-0058)}\td{Sector product laws and equivariant elimination at mass #{a=m^2>0}. A symmetry-stable retained set may induce a new law. The algebraic limit #{a=0} is not a normalized massless law.}}
    \tr{\td{B1}\td{[0059](fdip-0059) to [005I](fdip-005I)}\td{A selected-background tuple and the Gaussian law on the image of its projector. One quaternion action constructs a projector. It is not an extra slot of the tuple.}}
    \tr{\td{B2}\td{[005J](fdip-005J) to [005P](fdip-005P)}\td{Restriction of a selected law is equivalent to invariance of the precision, including the case of a repeated eigenvalue. There is no complete spectral classification.}}
    \tr{\td{B3}\td{[005Q](fdip-005Q) to [005X](fdip-005X)}\td{The rooted-forest expansion of #{\det(L_w+m^2I)}, the edge law, and #{\det(L_w+m^2I)=n\tau_w m^2+O(m^4)}. The unscaled massless determinant tends to zero.}}
    \tr{\td{B4}\td{[005Y](fdip-005Y) to [0062](fdip-0062)}\td{The conditional interaction of a general selector, and formal quartic vertices on an alternating cycle. No convergence of the formal series is claimed. The numerical value in [0061](fdip-0061) is one conditional moment.}}
    \tr{\td{B5}\td{[0063](fdip-0063) to [006B](fdip-006B)}\td{Conditional independence from a block precision, and chordal gluing along a junction tree. The four-cycle is an obstruction. There is no stack-gluing theorem.}}
    \tr{\td{B6}\td{[006C](fdip-006C) to [006K](fdip-006K)}\td{Gauge covariance of an invariant-metric connection, massive positivity #{Q_U\ge m^2I}, and the twisted cycle determinant. The connection is a fixed background.}}
    \tr{\td{G1a}\td{[006L](fdip-006L) to [006T](fdip-006T)}\td{The exact pure-gauge #{Z_2} law on a finite two-dimensional torus, including its area law at fixed #{\beta>0}. The checked torus is #{3\times 3}.}}
    \tr{\td{G1b}\td{[006U](fdip-006U) to [006Y](fdip-006Y)}\td{The normalized character formula on a closed oriented surface, checked for the quaternion group and the binary tetrahedral group. This is a finite sum.}}
    \tr{\td{G2}\td{[006Z](fdip-006Z) to [0074](fdip-0074)}\td{A hopping expansion for matter in a fixed unitary connection, on the stated unit-weight lattice, under the periods-at-least-6 condition.}}
    \tr{\td{G3}\td{[0075](fdip-0075) to [0079](fdip-0079)}\td{A joint gauge and Gaussian law at finite volume. In the checked example the matter determinant is smallest at trivial holonomy. No general matter-coupled area law is claimed.}}
    \tr{\td{G4}\td{[007A](fdip-007A) to [007K](fdip-007K)}\td{Compact #{U(1)} and #{SU(2)} data, the Wilson and Villain coefficients, and a classical small-spacing expansion. No quantum continuum limit is claimed.}}
    \tr{\td{T1}\td{[007L](fdip-007L) to [007R](fdip-007R)}\td{Scalar reflection positivity for the stated Gaussian class. The two-site Gram matrix in [007P](fdip-007P) shows that reflection positivity alone does not produce a translation operator.}}
    \tr{\td{T2a}\td{[007S](fdip-007S) to [007X](fdip-007X)}\td{The Mehler transfer operator of the massive Gaussian chain, for #{w,m^2>0}, and the trace correlation formula on its stated finite domain.}}
    \tr{\td{T2b}\td{[007Y](fdip-007Y) to [0087](fdip-0087)}\td{The finite-volume pure-gauge transfer operator, built from positive summable Fourier coefficients. Reflection positivity is not the hypothesis. No vacuum or continuum reconstruction is claimed.}}
    \tr{\td{T3}\td{[0088](fdip-0088) to [008D](fdip-008D)}\td{Charged physical states are invariant tensors. They are not the whole isotypic component. The checked dimensions are the stated symmetric-group and multigraph counts.}}
    \tr{\td{F1}\td{[008E](fdip-008E) to [008L](fdip-008L)}\td{Algebraic Berezin integration, the determinant and Pfaffian, and the odd Wick identities. This integration is separated from positive measures.}}
    \tr{\td{F2}\td{[009I](fdip-009I) to [009M](fdip-009M)}\td{Fermionic elimination when the eliminated block is invertible. On the accretive cone the bosonic integral is a complex weight. A positive law is a further hypothesis.}}
    \tr{\td{F3}\td{[009N](fdip-009N) to [009S](fdip-009S)}\td{Covariance of the lattice Dirac operator when the gauge acts on the internal factor and commutes with the Clifford action. Free and twisted kernels are counted. A spin-frame transformation is a further hypothesis.}}
    \tr{\td{F4}\td{[009T](fdip-009T) to [009X](fdip-009X)}\td{For the free Wilson operator, only the zero-momentum block remains in the kernel. [009W](fdip-009W) keeps the Hermitian-convention counterexample. The identity is a free-field statement.}}
    \tr{\td{W1}\td{[009Y](fdip-009Y) to [00A1](fdip-00A1)}\td{The finite maps #{SU(2)\to SO(3)} and #{2T\to A_4}, each with kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. Exactness and the named selector identity are proved over #{\mathbb R} after the realification in [00A1](fdip-00A1).}}
    \tr{\td{W2}\td{[00A2](fdip-00A2) to [00A4](fdip-00A4)}\td{Open tests. The source claims are stated in [00A3](fdip-00A3) and [00A4](fdip-00A4). No proposal is adopted, and a missing map is not a no-go theorem.}}
  }
}

\p{\strong{Deferred items.} These were outside the twenty-five
background units. None of them was proved.}

\table{
  \thead{\tr{\th{Item}\th{Status}}}
  \tbody{
    \tr{\td{Higher-dimensional strong-coupling area law}\td{Not done. The area law of [006R](fdip-006R) is pure gauge, two-dimensional, and at fixed #{\beta>0}. A leading-order discussion in the compact-group chapter is not this theorem.}}
    \tr{\td{Dirac–Kähler comparison}\td{Not done. [0091](fdip-0091) is still a planned chapter. A later discrete-calculus note may take up the cochain route. That plan is not a proof.}}
    \tr{\td{Fermion reflection positivity and fermion transfer}\td{Not done. The Wilson chapter allows such a remark only with the source hypotheses stated, and no such remark is supplied.}}
    \tr{\td{Infinite-volume or continuum reconstruction}\td{Not done. The finite transfer operator does not claim an Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction, an infinite-volume limit, or a continuum limit of an interacting fermion or gauge-matter law.}}
  }
}

\p{\strong{Wilson's claims.} The tested claims are the two finite
maps of [00A0](fdip-00A0): conjugation #{SU(2)\to SO(3)} and the
quotient #{2T\to A_4}, both with kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. The larger
proposals in [00A2](fdip-00A2) stay open. Three of the four Lie
names do not occur in the cited Wilson paper, and the Hessian
and #{SL(4,\mathbb R)} claims are recorded there as
interpretations. The absence of a construction is not a no-go
theorem and not a physical identification.}

\p{\strong{Next questions.} Four questions remain after this
accounting. The [physical environment map](fdip-003X) #{E} is
still unconstructed. The continuum comparison is still the one
fixed Fourier mode of [004J](fdip-004J); the deferred rows above
say what has not been added, and the question of continuous
space at large scales is left to a separate plan. The
[enriched background groupoid](fdip-000X) and the
[geometric realization](fdip-004L) still do not supply a site, a
stack, or a descent. Wilson's finite maps are tested, and the
larger proposals remain open tests.}

\transclude{fdip-009H}
